  "live_records": {
    "q-84679": {
      "dom_id": "step-q84679",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "A bolt manufacturer determines that $1.5%$ of the bolts produced are defective. A quality control inspection will check $200$ randomly selected bolts. What is the mean number of defective bolts in the sample?",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of defective bolts in the sample.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=200$ bolts\n\n- the probability of selecting a defective bolt is $p=1.5%=0.015$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(200,0.015).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the mean is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the mean number of defective bolts is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =(200)(0.015) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
