    "q-89405": {
      "dom_id": "step-q89405",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "A student answers a true-false test choosing each answer at random. If the test has $17$ questions, what is the mean of the number of correct answers?",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of correct answers.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=17$ questions\n\n- the probability of selecting the correct answer in each question is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(17,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the mean is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the mean number of correct answers is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =17\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =8.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
