    "q-84694": {
      "dom_id": "step-q84694",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "A coffee shop has determined that $35%$ of its customers buy a piece of cake when visiting. A random sample of $40$ customers is taken. What is the variance in the number of customers that bought a piece of cake?",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of clients that bought a piece of cake.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=40$ customers\n\n- the probability of a customer buying a piece of cake is $p=35%=0.35$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(40,0.35).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\n So, the variance of the number of clients that bought a piece of cake is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =(40)(0.35)(1-0.35) \\\\  & =(14)(0.65) \\\\  & =9.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
