    "q-130765": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130765",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Three doors in a computer game hide an apple, and a fourth door hides an orange. Fifteen players are playing the game, and each player is asked to choose one door at random.  What is the variance of the number of players winning an apple?",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of players winning an apple.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=15$ players in the game\n\n- the probability of choosing a door that hides an apple is $p=\\frac{3}{4}$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(15,\\frac{3}{4}).$ \n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the variance of the number of players winning an apple is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (1-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
