    "q-84494": {
      "dom_id": "step-q84494",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "A fair six-sided die is rolled $12$ times. What is the variance of the number of times we get a number less than $3?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of time we get a number less than $3.$\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ rolls\n\n- the probability of getting a number less than $3$ (i.e., a $1$ or $2$) on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,\\frac{1}{3}).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the variance of the number of times we get a number less than $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
