    "q-89412": {
      "dom_id": "step-q89412",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Approximately $20%$ of the emails received in an account are spam. If there are $20$ inbox emails, what is the standard deviation of the number of spam emails received? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of spam emails.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=20$ inbox emails\n\n- the probability of being a spam email is $p=20%=0.20$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(20,0.20).$ \n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of spam emails received is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(20)(0.20)(1-0.20)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3.2} \\\\  & =1.789\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
