    "q-130886": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130886",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "A fair twelve-sided die with sides labeled with numbers $1$ through $12$ is rolled $80$ times. What is the standard deviation of the number of times we get a number divisible by $5?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of times we get a number divisible by $5.$\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=80$ rolls\n\n- the probability of getting a number divisible by $5$ (i.e., a $5$ or a $10$) on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{12}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(80,\\frac{1}{6}).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the  number of times we get a number divisible by $5$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (1-\\frac{1}{6})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{100}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
