    "q-89411": {
      "dom_id": "step-q89411",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "A student answers a multiple-choice test choosing each answer at random. If the test has $16$ questions, and each question has $4$ possible answers with only one correct answer, what is the standard deviation of the number of correct answers? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of correct answers.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=16$ questions\n\n- the probability of selecting the correct answer in each question is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(16,\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of correct answers is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =1.732\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
